# Deployed to ~/.config/power-user-linux-setup/proxy.env by `inv proxy.install --keyring-fallback`.
# Read by pulse-proxy.service's EnvironmentFile=- and sourced by pulse-proxy-start.sh, so exactly
# two processes see it: Px, and PULSE's own one-shot keyring writer.
#
# It pins the backend for those processes only. The alternative, ~/.config/python_keyring/
# keyringrc.cfg, is global to every keyring consumer on the machine and would quietly downgrade
# unrelated tools to a plaintext store — including after a real Secret Service appeared.
#
# Never a credential. This names the store; the credential is inside it. With this backend that
# store is ~/.local/share/python_keyring/keyring_pass.cfg, a 0600 file holding a base64-encoded
# password that anything running as this user can read — see docs/corporate-proxy.md, "When there
# is no keyring", for why that is a flag rather than an automatic fallback.
PYTHON_KEYRING_BACKEND=keyrings.alt.file.PlaintextKeyring
